From: gstreamer-custom-build local patches
Subject: [PATCH] mpegpsdemux: base the timeline on the first PTS when no SCR has been seen

mpegpsdemux takes the media time that maps to running time zero from the first
SCR it parses out of a pack header:

    if (G_UNLIKELY (demux->first_scr == G_MAXUINT64)) {
        demux->first_scr = scr;
        demux->base_time = MPEGTIME_TO_GSTTIME (demux->first_scr);

and gst_ps_demux_send_segment() shifts the segment it sends by that base_time,
so the segment start lands next to the PTS of the buffers and their running time
comes out near zero. That is correct, and for a file it is always what happens:
a PS file begins with a pack header.

A live PS stream joined mid-flight need not. When the first PES packet arrives
before the first pack header, base_time is still G_MAXUINT64 when the segment
goes out, the shift is skipped, and a segment starting at zero is sent for
buffers whose PTS is the sender's own clock. Every frame then has a running time
of tens of thousands of seconds, and there is nothing downstream can do with
that: a synced sink waits for a time that will not come for eleven hours, and a
live aggregator holds the frame - and, for a hardware decoder, the surface
behind it - while its pool allocates a fresh one for every frame that follows.

Measured on an Intel N150 receiving 720x480i MPEG-2 PS over RTP (RFC 2250,
payload type 96) from two senders, joining at an arbitrary point each time:

  base_time found     segment start 39831.988 s, pts 39832.803 s -> running 0.815 s
  base_time not found segment start     0.000 s, pts 37872.600 s -> running 37872.600 s

Which of the two happens is a race with the sender's pack header interval, and
it decided the outcome per start-up: of ten starts of the same application
against the same two streams, the epoch leaked through on six.

Downstream cannot repair it either, and the attempts are instructive.
`identity single-segment=true` rewrites the segment start to zero and leaves the
buffer timestamps alone, which produces exactly the same running time.
`ignore-scr=true` does not touch the timing at all: that path updates
first_scr/current_scr and returns before base_time is ever assigned. Correcting
it with a pad offset in the application works until the offset has to change,
and then the frames in flight straddle two mappings.

So take the base from the first timestamped buffer when no SCR has provided one.
The function already receives that PTS and already logs it; it just did not use
it. A pack header arriving later sets first_scr for the SCR bookkeeping as
before and leaves base_time alone, since it is no longer G_MAXUINT64.

--- a/_p2_/gst/mpegdemux/gstmpegdemux.c
+++ b/_p2_/gst/mpegdemux/gstmpegdemux.c
@@ -664,6 +664,20 @@
         GST_TIME_ARGS (demux->src_segment.start),
         GST_TIME_ARGS (demux->src_segment.stop));
 
+    /* Local fix: a live stream joined between two pack headers has no SCR yet,
+     * so base_time is unset and the shift below is skipped - a segment starting
+     * at zero goes out for buffers carrying the sender's own clock, and every
+     * frame then has a running time of tens of thousands of seconds. The first
+     * timestamped buffer is as good a base as the first SCR: it is the media
+     * time that is meant to play now. A pack header arriving later still sets
+     * first_scr for the SCR bookkeeping and leaves this alone. */
+    if (!GST_CLOCK_TIME_IS_VALID (demux->base_time)
+        && GST_CLOCK_TIME_IS_VALID (pts)) {
+      demux->base_time = pts;
+      GST_DEBUG_OBJECT (demux, "no SCR yet, taking base_time from the first "
+          "PTS: %" GST_TIME_FORMAT, GST_TIME_ARGS (demux->base_time));
+    }
+
     /* we should be in sync with downstream, so start from our segment notion,
      * which also includes proper base_time etc, tweak it a bit and send */
     gst_segment_copy_into (&demux->src_segment, &segment);
